1
00:00:00,275 --> 00:00:02,200
This is a black hole that spins.

2
00:00:02,200 --> 00:00:04,212
It isn't an artist's impression.

3
00:00:04,212 --> 00:00:07,575
Every pixel is a ray of light, traced backward from the camera through the

4
00:00:07,575 --> 00:00:12,838
geometry of a rotating black hole, the solution Roy Kerr found in 1963, until

5
00:00:12,838 --> 00:00:17,738
it hits a thin disk of hot gas, falls in, or escapes to the distant stars.

6
00:00:17,738 --> 00:00:21,450
The gas on the left is coming toward us, so it's brighter and bluer.

7
00:00:21,450 --> 00:00:25,538
And the dark region, the shadow, is flattened on one side by the rotation,

8
00:00:25,538 --> 00:00:29,025
exactly as the theory predicts.

9
00:00:29,725 --> 00:00:33,863
In part one, we derived Einstein's field equation from scratch, and solved

10
00:00:33,863 --> 00:00:35,425
it outside a star.

11
00:00:35,425 --> 00:00:39,625
With that solution, we explained the orbit of Mercury and the bending of light,

12
00:00:39,625 --> 00:00:43,200
and we saw that ripples in spacetime travel at the speed of light.

13
00:00:43,200 --> 00:00:46,175
But we stopped at the edge of the most interesting place.

14
00:00:46,175 --> 00:00:51,363
At r equals two G M over c squared, the Schwarzschild metric misbehaves:

15
00:00:51,363 --> 00:00:56,450
a clock there stops, as seen from far away, and the radial part blows up.

16
00:00:56,450 --> 00:01:00,463
We claimed that inside, every future direction points inward.

17
00:01:00,463 --> 00:01:04,975
This time we'll make that precise, and go much further.

18
00:01:05,175 --> 00:01:06,425
Here's the plan.

19
00:01:06,425 --> 00:01:09,750
First, we'll fall into a black hole, find coordinates that see through the

20
00:01:09,750 --> 00:01:14,550
horizon, draw the entire spacetime on a single page, and prove that singularities

21
00:01:14,550 --> 00:01:15,975
are unavoidable.

22
00:01:15,975 --> 00:01:17,588
Second, we'll make it spin:

23
00:01:17,588 --> 00:01:21,575
gyroscopes, the dragging of space, Kerr's solution, and how to mine energy

24
00:01:21,575 --> 00:01:23,700
from a black hole's rotation.

25
00:01:23,700 --> 00:01:26,650
Third, we'll derive what part one could only quote:

26
00:01:26,650 --> 00:01:30,750
the energy carried by gravitational waves, and the chirp of two black holes

27
00:01:30,750 --> 00:01:32,000
merging.

28
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:36,263
Fourth, we'll find that black holes obey the laws of thermodynamics, and have

29
00:01:36,263 --> 00:01:37,450
a temperature.

30
00:01:37,450 --> 00:01:43,000
And finally, we'll apply Einstein's equation to the universe as a whole.

31
00:01:43,183 --> 00:01:45,158
One piece of bookkeeping first.

32
00:01:45,158 --> 00:01:50,008
From here on, we'll use units in which G and c are both equal to one, as relativists

33
00:01:50,008 --> 00:01:50,796
do.

34
00:01:50,796 --> 00:01:53,833
Then a mass is also a length, and also a time:

35
00:01:53,833 --> 00:01:58,083
the Sun's mass is one point four eight kilometers, or four point nine three

36
00:01:58,083 --> 00:01:59,496
microseconds.

37
00:01:59,496 --> 00:02:03,508
The Schwarzschild metric loses its clutter, and the Schwarzschild radius is

38
00:02:03,508 --> 00:02:04,833
simply two M.

39
00:02:10,449 --> 00:02:13,962
Picture an astronaut stepping off a space station that hovers at r equals

40
00:02:13,962 --> 00:02:15,949
ten M, and falling straight in.

41
00:02:15,949 --> 00:02:17,599
We already have the tools.

42
00:02:17,599 --> 00:02:21,312
Because the metric doesn't change with time, the quantity E is conserved along

43
00:02:21,312 --> 00:02:22,099
the fall:

44
00:02:22,099 --> 00:02:25,574
it's the energy per unit mass, as measured from far away.

45
00:02:25,574 --> 00:02:28,599
And the four-velocity always has length minus one.

46
00:02:28,599 --> 00:02:32,574
Substituting the first equation into the second gives the radial speed.

47
00:02:32,574 --> 00:02:37,186
Starting from rest, it's exactly Newton's energy equation, with proper time

48
00:02:37,186 --> 00:02:39,249
playing the role of time.

49
00:02:39,249 --> 00:02:43,549
Its solution is a cycloid, written with a parameter eta.

50
00:02:43,549 --> 00:02:46,462
Nothing special happens at r equals two M.

51
00:02:46,462 --> 00:02:50,574
The astronaut's watch reads a finite time when they cross it, and a finite

52
00:02:50,574 --> 00:02:54,299
time when they reach r equals zero.

53
00:02:54,466 --> 00:02:56,466
Let's watch it, with two clocks.

54
00:02:56,466 --> 00:03:00,603
On top, the astronaut's distance from the center against their own watch:

55
00:03:00,603 --> 00:03:05,516
a smooth fall, through r equals two M, and on to zero, in about thirty-five

56
00:03:05,516 --> 00:03:05,716
M.

57
00:03:05,716 --> 00:03:10,053
Below, the same fall against the station's clock, which runs at the rate of

58
00:03:10,053 --> 00:03:11,241
coordinate time t.

59
00:03:11,241 --> 00:03:15,416
Here the astronaut slows down as they approach two M, and never quite gets

60
00:03:15,416 --> 00:03:16,216
there:

61
00:03:16,216 --> 00:03:18,491
t grows without bound.

62
00:03:18,491 --> 00:03:23,766
For a black hole of ten solar masses, M is forty-nine microseconds, so the

63
00:03:23,766 --> 00:03:28,541
whole trip takes under two milliseconds of the astronaut's time.

64
00:03:28,674 --> 00:03:30,611
So what does the station actually see?

65
00:03:30,611 --> 00:03:32,586
Draw it on a spacetime diagram:

66
00:03:32,586 --> 00:03:35,249
distance across, the station's time up.

67
00:03:35,249 --> 00:03:39,049
The astronaut's worldline bends over and runs up along r equals two M.

68
00:03:39,049 --> 00:03:43,199
Now suppose they send a flash of light back to the station every so often,

69
00:03:43,199 --> 00:03:44,586
by their own watch.

70
00:03:44,586 --> 00:03:47,361
The flashes arrive further and further apart.

71
00:03:47,361 --> 00:03:51,349
A flash sent exactly at r equals two M never arrives at all:

72
00:03:51,349 --> 00:03:55,674
that outgoing light ray stays at r equals two M forever.

73
00:03:55,674 --> 00:03:57,824
The light also arrives stretched.

74
00:03:57,824 --> 00:04:01,574
Its redshift grows, and at late times it grows exponentially:

75
00:04:01,574 --> 00:04:06,149
the wavelength increases by a factor of e every four M of the station's time.

76
00:04:06,149 --> 00:04:10,011
For a ten-solar-mass black hole, that's a fifth of a millisecond.

77
00:04:10,011 --> 00:04:14,411
To the station, the astronaut seems to freeze at the horizon, turn red, and

78
00:04:14,411 --> 00:04:18,761
fade from view almost instantly, while the astronaut, by their own watch,

79
00:04:18,761 --> 00:04:21,449
simply falls through.

80
00:04:21,632 --> 00:04:23,607
Two more facts about the inside.

81
00:04:23,607 --> 00:04:27,407
Once you've crossed, the longest you can possibly survive, by your own watch,

82
00:04:27,407 --> 00:04:30,782
is pi M, and firing rockets only shortens it.

83
00:04:30,782 --> 00:04:32,457
And what you feel is tides:

84
00:04:32,457 --> 00:04:36,370
the difference in acceleration across your body, two M over r cubed times

85
00:04:36,370 --> 00:04:37,345
your height.

86
00:04:37,345 --> 00:04:41,220
For a stellar black hole, at the horizon that's twenty million g's, and you'd

87
00:04:41,220 --> 00:04:43,532
be torn apart well before you reached it.

88
00:04:43,532 --> 00:04:46,382
But tides shrink with the square of the mass.

89
00:04:46,382 --> 00:04:50,695
At the horizon of the black hole in the center of our galaxy, they're a ten-thousandth

90
00:04:50,695 --> 00:04:56,020
of a g, and at the giant one in M87, utterly unnoticeable, and you'd have

91
00:04:56,020 --> 00:04:58,082
more than a day before the end.

92
00:04:58,082 --> 00:05:01,495
So locally, the horizon is an ordinary place.

93
00:05:01,495 --> 00:05:03,657
What breaks there is our coordinates.

94
00:05:05,305 --> 00:05:07,668
To find better coordinates, follow light.

95
00:05:07,668 --> 00:05:10,993
A light ray moving straight in or out has zero interval.

96
00:05:10,993 --> 00:05:14,680
So dt d r is plus or minus one over one minus two M over r.

97
00:05:14,680 --> 00:05:20,218
Integrating, t is plus or minus a new radial coordinate, r star, plus a constant.

98
00:05:20,218 --> 00:05:23,818
Physicists call r star the tortoise coordinate, for a reason you can see in

99
00:05:23,818 --> 00:05:24,955
its graph.

100
00:05:24,955 --> 00:05:29,118
As r approaches two M, r star plunges to minus infinity:

101
00:05:29,118 --> 00:05:35,205
measured in t, light takes forever to reach the horizon, like Zeno's tortoise.

102
00:05:35,364 --> 00:05:38,726
So label events by the ingoing light ray they sit on.

103
00:05:38,726 --> 00:05:41,376
Define v equals t plus r star;

104
00:05:41,376 --> 00:05:44,564
it's constant along every ingoing ray.

105
00:05:44,564 --> 00:05:48,339
Then dt is d v minus d r over one minus two M over r.

106
00:05:48,339 --> 00:05:51,839
Substitute that into the metric, and something lovely happens:

107
00:05:51,839 --> 00:05:56,076
the d r squared terms cancel exactly, and what's left has nothing that blows

108
00:05:56,076 --> 00:05:57,476
up at r equals two M.

109
00:05:57,476 --> 00:06:00,989
The determinant isn't zero there, and it's still a solution of Einstein's

110
00:06:00,989 --> 00:06:02,964
equation in empty space:

111
00:06:02,964 --> 00:06:06,176
the same spacetime, in better coordinates.

112
00:06:06,176 --> 00:06:11,014
Eddington wrote down this form in 1924, but didn't remark on it.

113
00:06:11,014 --> 00:06:17,089
David Finkelstein rediscovered it in 1958, and saw what it meant.

114
00:06:17,239 --> 00:06:19,226
Let's see what this does to the picture.

115
00:06:19,226 --> 00:06:20,964
Here's the Schwarzschild chart:

116
00:06:20,964 --> 00:06:24,389
r across, t up, with light cones at a few places.

117
00:06:24,389 --> 00:06:28,951
Near r equals two M, the cones get narrower and narrower, and close up.

118
00:06:28,951 --> 00:06:31,451
That's what made the horizon look like a wall.

119
00:06:31,451 --> 00:06:36,014
Now change the time coordinate to t tilde, which is v minus r, so that ingoing

120
00:06:36,014 --> 00:06:38,726
light always moves at forty-five degrees.

121
00:06:38,726 --> 00:06:39,964
Watch the cones.

122
00:06:39,964 --> 00:06:41,789
They stop closing.

123
00:06:41,789 --> 00:06:45,989
Instead, as you approach the horizon, they tip over, toward smaller r.

124
00:06:45,989 --> 00:06:51,176
The lines of constant t, which seemed so natural, plunge down at the horizon:

125
00:06:51,176 --> 00:06:54,489
they were the problem all along.

126
00:06:54,689 --> 00:06:57,201
Now draw the black hole in these coordinates.

127
00:06:57,201 --> 00:07:00,951
Ingoing light rays are straight lines at forty-five degrees.

128
00:07:00,951 --> 00:07:05,114
Outgoing rays, launched outside the horizon, escape, though the closer they

129
00:07:05,114 --> 00:07:07,451
start to it, the longer they linger.

130
00:07:07,451 --> 00:07:11,739
A ray launched outward exactly at r equals two M stays there forever:

131
00:07:11,739 --> 00:07:15,589
the horizon is itself made of light rays, hovering in place.

132
00:07:15,589 --> 00:07:20,814
And inside, even a ray aimed outward moves to smaller r, and ends at r equals

133
00:07:20,814 --> 00:07:22,172
zero.

134
00:07:22,172 --> 00:07:25,085
Here's our astronaut again, falling from the station.

135
00:07:25,085 --> 00:07:29,122
In these coordinates, their worldline crosses the horizon smoothly, at a finite

136
00:07:29,122 --> 00:07:29,935
time.

137
00:07:29,935 --> 00:07:32,135
Follow their light cone along the way.

138
00:07:32,135 --> 00:07:35,022
Outside, part of the future still points outward.

139
00:07:35,022 --> 00:07:38,422
At the horizon, the outward edge of the cone points straight up.

140
00:07:38,422 --> 00:07:42,385
Inside, the entire future points toward r equals zero.

141
00:07:42,385 --> 00:07:44,610
The horizon is a one-way membrane:

142
00:07:44,610 --> 00:07:47,822
things can cross it inward, and nothing can cross it outward.

143
00:07:49,495 --> 00:07:53,133
Finkelstein's coordinates see through the horizon, but only for things falling

144
00:07:53,133 --> 00:07:53,908
in.

145
00:07:53,908 --> 00:07:57,245
To see the whole spacetime, use both kinds of light rays.

146
00:07:57,245 --> 00:08:01,995
With u equal to t minus r star, constant along outgoing rays, the metric is

147
00:08:01,995 --> 00:08:05,445
just minus one minus two M over r, times d u d v.

148
00:08:05,445 --> 00:08:08,383
But u runs off to infinity at the horizon.

149
00:08:08,383 --> 00:08:09,495
So squeeze it:

150
00:08:09,495 --> 00:08:12,908
define capital U and V as exponentials of u and v.

151
00:08:12,908 --> 00:08:18,254
Their product depends only on r, and vanishes exactly at the horizon.

152
00:08:18,254 --> 00:08:23,504
In U and V, the metric's factor is finite, and nonzero, at r equals two M.

153
00:08:23,504 --> 00:08:27,829
And with T and X, the sum and difference, it's the flat metric of special

154
00:08:27,829 --> 00:08:30,591
relativity times a positive factor.

155
00:08:30,591 --> 00:08:34,729
So light travels at forty-five degrees everywhere in this picture.

156
00:08:34,729 --> 00:08:39,629
These are the coordinates Martin Kruskal and George Szekeres found, independently,

157
00:08:39,629 --> 00:08:42,479
in 1960.

158
00:08:42,637 --> 00:08:45,775
Let's see how the familiar picture fits inside this one.

159
00:08:45,775 --> 00:08:49,375
Here's the outside of the black hole in Schwarzschild's coordinates:

160
00:08:49,375 --> 00:08:53,125
blue lines of constant r, teal lines of constant t.

161
00:08:53,125 --> 00:08:56,912
Now move every point to its place in Kruskal's coordinates.

162
00:08:56,912 --> 00:08:59,912
Lines of constant r become hyperbolas.

163
00:08:59,912 --> 00:09:04,300
Lines of constant t become straight lines through the origin, like the spokes

164
00:09:04,300 --> 00:09:05,687
of a fan.

165
00:09:05,687 --> 00:09:10,375
All of Schwarzschild time, from minus infinity to plus infinity, fits inside

166
00:09:10,375 --> 00:09:14,687
a wedge, and the edges of the wedge, where t is infinite, are the lines X

167
00:09:14,687 --> 00:09:16,837
equals plus or minus T:

168
00:09:16,837 --> 00:09:19,337
the horizon.

169
00:09:19,470 --> 00:09:21,820
And now we can see what lies beyond.

170
00:09:21,820 --> 00:09:25,620
The horizon is the pair of diagonal lines, made of light rays, just as we

171
00:09:25,620 --> 00:09:26,633
found.

172
00:09:26,633 --> 00:09:30,808
Above them is the inside of the black hole, region two, where lines of constant

173
00:09:30,808 --> 00:09:35,195
r are hyperbolas again, but now horizontal ones, ending at the singularity,

174
00:09:35,195 --> 00:09:38,233
r equals zero, which is also a hyperbola.

175
00:09:38,233 --> 00:09:42,183
The equations, solved in full, also contain two more regions:

176
00:09:42,183 --> 00:09:46,320
a mirror-image exterior on the left, and below, a white hole, a region that

177
00:09:46,320 --> 00:09:48,245
things can only leave.

178
00:09:48,245 --> 00:09:52,087
This is the maximally extended Schwarzschild spacetime.

179
00:09:52,087 --> 00:09:56,525
Light cones are forty-five degrees everywhere, so causality is easy to read.

180
00:09:56,525 --> 00:09:58,262
Here's our astronaut's worldline:

181
00:09:58,262 --> 00:10:01,825
it crosses the horizon smoothly, and ends on the singularity.

182
00:10:01,825 --> 00:10:03,750
Look at their light cone inside.

183
00:10:03,750 --> 00:10:06,412
Every direction in it hits the singularity.

184
00:10:06,412 --> 00:10:10,550
Inside the black hole, the lines of constant r run sideways:

185
00:10:10,550 --> 00:10:12,862
r has become a time coordinate.

186
00:10:12,862 --> 00:10:15,712
The singularity isn't a place you could steer around.

187
00:10:15,712 --> 00:10:20,887
It's a moment in your future, as unavoidable as next Tuesday.

188
00:10:21,037 --> 00:10:23,475
This picture also contains a wormhole.

189
00:10:23,475 --> 00:10:25,687
Take a slice of constant Kruskal time T.

190
00:10:25,687 --> 00:10:30,025
At T equals zero, it runs from the other exterior on the left, through r equals

191
00:10:30,025 --> 00:10:32,337
two M, to ours on the right.

192
00:10:32,337 --> 00:10:36,137
Its geometry, embedded as a surface, is two copies of Flamm's funnel from

193
00:10:36,137 --> 00:10:38,262
part one, joined at the throat:

194
00:10:38,262 --> 00:10:42,062
the Einstein–Rosen bridge, a tunnel between two universes.

195
00:10:42,062 --> 00:10:43,575
But it isn't static.

196
00:10:43,575 --> 00:10:48,762
Move the slice up in T, and the throat shrinks, and pinches off, at the singularity.

197
00:10:48,762 --> 00:10:52,587
Running backward, it was born from the white hole's singularity.

198
00:10:52,587 --> 00:10:56,475
It closes too fast for anything, even light, to get through:

199
00:10:56,475 --> 00:11:00,079
a light ray sent across ends on the singularity.

200
00:11:00,079 --> 00:11:01,529
Is any of this real?

201
00:11:01,529 --> 00:11:05,629
The extra regions are there because Schwarzschild's solution describes a black

202
00:11:05,629 --> 00:11:07,979
hole that has existed forever.

203
00:11:07,979 --> 00:11:12,579
Real black holes form from collapsing stars, and to see what that changes,

204
00:11:12,579 --> 00:11:15,154
we need one more way of drawing spacetime.

205
00:11:16,776 --> 00:11:20,764
Kruskal's picture still runs off to infinity in every direction.

206
00:11:20,764 --> 00:11:25,226
Roger Penrose found a way to fit all of spacetime, infinity included, on a

207
00:11:25,226 --> 00:11:26,626
finite page.

208
00:11:26,626 --> 00:11:30,114
Start with flat spacetime, in the null coordinates u and v.

209
00:11:30,114 --> 00:11:34,814
Squeeze each of them with an arctangent, so their infinite ranges become finite.

210
00:11:34,814 --> 00:11:38,001
The metric picks up a factor that blows up at the edges.

211
00:11:38,001 --> 00:11:38,901
Now the trick:

212
00:11:38,901 --> 00:11:41,101
throw that factor away.

213
00:11:41,101 --> 00:11:46,026
Multiplying a metric by a positive function changes lengths, but not angles,

214
00:11:46,026 --> 00:11:50,176
so light cones keep their shape, and the causal structure, who can signal

215
00:11:50,176 --> 00:11:52,564
whom, is exactly preserved.

216
00:11:52,564 --> 00:11:57,601
What's left is a finite picture of an infinite spacetime.

217
00:11:57,735 --> 00:12:00,022
Here's what that does to flat spacetime.

218
00:12:00,022 --> 00:12:04,797
A grid of lines, one per unit of time and of distance, stretching off forever.

219
00:12:04,797 --> 00:12:06,135
Apply the squeeze.

220
00:12:06,135 --> 00:12:09,372
The whole infinite half-plane folds into a triangle.

221
00:12:09,372 --> 00:12:13,272
Every line of constant r runs from one point at the bottom to one point at

222
00:12:13,272 --> 00:12:14,110
the top:

223
00:12:14,110 --> 00:12:17,860
that's where every inertial observer comes from, and goes to.

224
00:12:17,860 --> 00:12:22,710
Lines of constant t all meet at the far corner, spatial infinity.

225
00:12:22,710 --> 00:12:26,672
And the two diagonal edges are where light rays come from and go to, called

226
00:12:26,672 --> 00:12:29,035
scri minus and scri plus.

227
00:12:29,035 --> 00:12:33,047
Light still moves at forty-five degrees, so you can read off at a glance what

228
00:12:33,047 --> 00:12:35,735
can influence what.

229
00:12:35,918 --> 00:12:37,931
Now do the same to the black hole.

230
00:12:37,931 --> 00:12:42,281
Start from Kruskal's diagram, and squeeze U and V with arctangents.

231
00:12:42,281 --> 00:12:46,756
The exterior becomes a diamond, the black hole a triangle on top, and the

232
00:12:46,756 --> 00:12:51,506
singularity, which was a hyperbola, becomes a straight horizontal line:

233
00:12:51,506 --> 00:12:56,768
r equals zero is where the arctangents of U and V add up to pi over two.

234
00:12:56,768 --> 00:13:00,168
This is the Penrose diagram of the Schwarzschild black hole.

235
00:13:00,168 --> 00:13:03,681
Our universe is the diamond on the right, with its own infinities:

236
00:13:03,681 --> 00:13:08,681
scri plus, where outgoing light ends up, and future and past timelike infinity.

237
00:13:08,681 --> 00:13:10,043
Here's the astronaut.

238
00:13:10,043 --> 00:13:13,043
And now the definition of a black hole becomes visual:

239
00:13:13,043 --> 00:13:16,543
it's the region from which no light ray can reach scri plus.

240
00:13:16,543 --> 00:13:18,193
Light from here gets out.

241
00:13:18,193 --> 00:13:23,618
Light from inside the horizon can only go up, into the singularity.

242
00:13:23,710 --> 00:13:25,935
But real black holes weren't always there:

243
00:13:25,935 --> 00:13:27,997
they form when a star collapses.

244
00:13:27,997 --> 00:13:32,097
Here's a star that sits at r equals five M and then collapses.

245
00:13:32,097 --> 00:13:36,535
Outside its surface, by Birkhoff's theorem, the geometry is exactly Schwarzschild's,

246
00:13:36,535 --> 00:13:38,910
so this part of the picture is exact.

247
00:13:38,910 --> 00:13:42,210
Inside is the star itself, not empty space.

248
00:13:42,210 --> 00:13:46,060
So the left half of Kruskal's diagram, with the white hole, the other universe

249
00:13:46,060 --> 00:13:50,735
and the wormhole, is simply covered up by the star, and never exists.

250
00:13:50,735 --> 00:13:54,335
The standard Penrose diagram of a collapse looks like this:

251
00:13:54,335 --> 00:13:58,410
the star's surface falls through the horizon and meets the singularity, and

252
00:13:58,410 --> 00:14:01,668
outside, everything is as before.

253
00:14:01,668 --> 00:14:04,043
And look at where the event horizon is.

254
00:14:04,043 --> 00:14:08,056
It's the boundary of the region that can never send light to scri plus, and

255
00:14:08,056 --> 00:14:12,081
it starts at the center of the star, before the surface has reached r equals

256
00:14:12,081 --> 00:14:13,156
two M:

257
00:14:13,156 --> 00:14:17,318
a horizon grows outward from the center, through matter that hasn't yet noticed

258
00:14:17,318 --> 00:14:18,431
anything.

259
00:14:18,431 --> 00:14:23,218
To know where the event horizon is today, you need to know the entire future.

260
00:14:23,218 --> 00:14:25,401
Nothing local marks it.

261
00:14:25,401 --> 00:14:27,289
That leaves the singularity.

262
00:14:27,289 --> 00:14:30,564
Every picture so far has been perfectly spherical.

263
00:14:30,564 --> 00:14:34,951
Maybe, in a real, lumpy collapse, the matter would miss the center and bounce

264
00:14:34,951 --> 00:14:35,926
back out?

265
00:14:35,926 --> 00:14:38,326
For decades, many physicists thought so.

266
00:14:39,966 --> 00:14:45,428
In 1963, two Soviet physicists, Lifshitz and Khalatnikov, argued that the

267
00:14:45,428 --> 00:14:48,128
singularity was an artifact of symmetry:

268
00:14:48,128 --> 00:14:53,303
in a realistic, irregular collapse, they concluded, no singularity would form.

269
00:14:53,303 --> 00:14:56,628
Two years later, Roger Penrose proved the opposite.

270
00:14:56,628 --> 00:15:00,891
Once a certain kind of surface forms, a trapped surface, a singularity is

271
00:15:00,891 --> 00:15:03,491
unavoidable, whatever the symmetry.

272
00:15:03,491 --> 00:15:09,091
The key tool is an equation found in 1955 by Amal Kumar Raychaudhuri, about

273
00:15:09,091 --> 00:15:15,641
how a bundle of free particles, or light rays, spreads out or converges.

274
00:15:15,741 --> 00:15:19,853
Picture a whole family of freely falling worldlines, side by side, like the

275
00:15:19,853 --> 00:15:22,328
ball of test particles from part one.

276
00:15:22,328 --> 00:15:26,366
Let u be their four-velocity, and B the tensor that says how u changes across

277
00:15:26,366 --> 00:15:27,278
the bundle:

278
00:15:27,278 --> 00:15:30,303
it tells you how the separation between neighbors grows.

279
00:15:30,303 --> 00:15:33,516
B splits into three parts, each with its own meaning.

280
00:15:33,516 --> 00:15:38,016
Its trace, theta, the expansion, says how fast the bundle's volume grows.

281
00:15:38,016 --> 00:15:42,303
Its symmetric, traceless part, the shear sigma, changes the shape without

282
00:15:42,303 --> 00:15:44,116
changing the volume.

283
00:15:44,116 --> 00:15:49,166
And its antisymmetric part, the rotation omega, twists it.

284
00:15:49,349 --> 00:15:52,574
Now let's derive how theta changes along the bundle.

285
00:15:52,574 --> 00:15:56,299
The rate of change of B is u dotted into a second covariant derivative of

286
00:15:56,299 --> 00:15:56,612
u.

287
00:15:56,612 --> 00:15:59,012
Swap the order of the two derivatives.

288
00:15:59,012 --> 00:16:03,274
Their commutator, as we found in part one, is the Riemann tensor.

289
00:16:03,274 --> 00:16:05,712
Use the product rule on the first term.

290
00:16:05,712 --> 00:16:08,712
The worldlines are free, so they're geodesics:

291
00:16:08,712 --> 00:16:13,999
u dot grad u is zero, and what's left is B times B, and curvature.

292
00:16:13,999 --> 00:16:17,399
Take the trace, and Riemann contracts to the Ricci tensor.

293
00:16:17,399 --> 00:16:20,199
Finally, write B as its three parts:

294
00:16:20,199 --> 00:16:23,949
the trace gives a third of theta squared, the shear and rotation give their

295
00:16:23,949 --> 00:16:26,624
squares, and the cross terms vanish.

296
00:16:26,624 --> 00:16:30,249
This is Raychaudhuri's equation.

297
00:16:30,424 --> 00:16:31,962
Read it term by term.

298
00:16:31,962 --> 00:16:33,099
Theta squared:

299
00:16:33,099 --> 00:16:36,824
once a bundle starts converging, convergence feeds on itself.

300
00:16:36,824 --> 00:16:38,937
Shear always adds to the focusing.

301
00:16:38,937 --> 00:16:43,474
Rotation is the only term that pushes the other way, like a centrifugal force.

302
00:16:43,474 --> 00:16:45,937
And the last term is where gravity enters.

303
00:16:45,937 --> 00:16:50,099
By Einstein's equation, for a fluid it's four pi times rho plus three p.

304
00:16:50,099 --> 00:16:51,512
We've seen this before:

305
00:16:51,512 --> 00:16:55,174
it's exactly Baez and Bunn's statement about a ball of particles starting

306
00:16:55,174 --> 00:16:56,137
at rest.

307
00:16:56,137 --> 00:16:59,149
That was Raychaudhuri's equation all along.

308
00:16:59,149 --> 00:17:04,074
As long as rho plus three p is positive, which is true of all ordinary matter,

309
00:17:04,074 --> 00:17:05,612
gravity focuses.

310
00:17:05,612 --> 00:17:08,924
For light rays, the condition needed is even weaker:

311
00:17:08,924 --> 00:17:12,249
rho plus p non-negative.

312
00:17:12,449 --> 00:17:14,837
Now the key consequence, for light rays.

313
00:17:14,837 --> 00:17:18,537
Suppose there's no rotation, which is true for the light rays leaving any

314
00:17:18,537 --> 00:17:21,437
surface, and the energy condition holds.

315
00:17:21,437 --> 00:17:25,799
Then every term on the right is negative or zero, so d theta d lambda is at

316
00:17:25,799 --> 00:17:28,024
most minus one half theta squared.

317
00:17:28,024 --> 00:17:30,574
Divide by theta squared and integrate.

318
00:17:30,574 --> 00:17:34,724
If the bundle starts out converging, with theta naught negative, then theta

319
00:17:34,724 --> 00:17:38,812
must reach minus infinity within an affine distance two over the magnitude

320
00:17:38,812 --> 00:17:40,349
of theta naught.

321
00:17:40,349 --> 00:17:44,599
Here's that bound, and an example with matter, which gets there even sooner.

322
00:17:44,599 --> 00:17:47,049
Minus infinity means the rays cross:

323
00:17:47,049 --> 00:17:49,337
a caustic, a focal point.

324
00:17:49,337 --> 00:17:53,949
Gravity is a lens that only ever converges.

325
00:17:54,124 --> 00:17:56,399
So where would light rays start out converging?

326
00:17:56,399 --> 00:17:59,799
Take a sphere outside a black hole and set off a flash of light on it.

327
00:17:59,799 --> 00:18:03,699
The flash splits into two fronts, one moving out and one moving in.

328
00:18:03,699 --> 00:18:07,062
The inner front shrinks, but the outer one grows, as you'd expect.

329
00:18:07,062 --> 00:18:09,137
Now do the same inside the horizon.

330
00:18:09,137 --> 00:18:10,462
Both fronts shrink.

331
00:18:10,462 --> 00:18:14,899
Even the light aimed outward converges, because inside, r is a time and it

332
00:18:14,899 --> 00:18:16,387
only decreases.

333
00:18:16,387 --> 00:18:20,262
A closed surface whose outgoing and ingoing light both converge is called

334
00:18:20,262 --> 00:18:22,999
a trapped surface.

335
00:18:23,133 --> 00:18:24,983
Here is Penrose's theorem.

336
00:18:24,983 --> 00:18:29,133
If gravity focuses light, if space at one moment is infinite, and if a trapped

337
00:18:29,133 --> 00:18:32,908
surface forms, then some light ray must come to an end after a finite affine

338
00:18:32,908 --> 00:18:33,983
distance:

339
00:18:33,983 --> 00:18:36,658
spacetime has an edge, a singularity.

340
00:18:36,658 --> 00:18:38,058
The idea of the proof:

341
00:18:38,058 --> 00:18:42,583
the light rays leaving a trapped surface are all converging, so by Raychaudhuri,

342
00:18:42,583 --> 00:18:45,520
they all reach caustics within a finite distance.

343
00:18:45,520 --> 00:18:49,433
So the boundary of everything the surface can influence is finite, closed

344
00:18:49,433 --> 00:18:50,533
off.

345
00:18:50,533 --> 00:18:55,008
But that finite boundary would have to map onto the whole of an infinite space,

346
00:18:55,008 --> 00:18:59,541
which is impossible, unless some of those light rays simply end.

347
00:18:59,541 --> 00:19:03,591
Nothing in this argument needs symmetry, and a slightly deformed trapped surface

348
00:19:03,591 --> 00:19:06,728
is still trapped, so the conclusion is robust.

349
00:19:06,728 --> 00:19:11,091
In 2020, Penrose received the Nobel Prize for exactly this:

350
00:19:11,091 --> 00:19:15,991
showing that black hole formation is a robust prediction of general relativity.

351
00:19:15,991 --> 00:19:19,416
Stephen Hawking turned the same argument around in time:

352
00:19:19,416 --> 00:19:23,491
an expanding universe filled with ordinary matter must have a singularity

353
00:19:23,491 --> 00:19:27,141
in its past, a precise version of the Big Bang.

354
00:19:27,141 --> 00:19:30,853
What the theorems don't say is what the singularity is like.

355
00:19:30,853 --> 00:19:35,241
They predict where general relativity itself breaks down, and a quantum theory

356
00:19:35,241 --> 00:19:36,891
of gravity must take over.

357
00:19:42,130 --> 00:19:46,467
In part one, Mercury and the bending of light came from a mass sitting still.

358
00:19:46,467 --> 00:19:49,392
What changes when the mass moves, or spins?

359
00:19:49,392 --> 00:19:51,942
Go back to the linearized field equation.

360
00:19:51,942 --> 00:19:55,880
For a static source, the time-time part gives Newton's potential.

361
00:19:55,880 --> 00:20:00,105
But a moving source also has momentum, and mass currents feed the time-space

362
00:20:00,105 --> 00:20:04,367
part of the metric, through exactly the equation electric currents use to

363
00:20:04,367 --> 00:20:06,930
make a magnetic vector potential.

364
00:20:06,930 --> 00:20:11,205
Far from a spinning body, the solution has the shape of a magnetic dipole's

365
00:20:11,205 --> 00:20:17,580
field, with the angular momentum J playing the role of the magnetic moment.

366
00:20:17,747 --> 00:20:20,522
This gravitomagnetic field has two effects.

367
00:20:20,522 --> 00:20:22,734
First, it drags things around.

368
00:20:22,734 --> 00:20:26,447
Drop something with zero angular momentum near a spinning body, and it doesn't

369
00:20:26,447 --> 00:20:27,559
fall straight in:

370
00:20:27,559 --> 00:20:31,547
it circles, with angular velocity two J over r cubed.

371
00:20:31,547 --> 00:20:35,097
Spacetime itself is being dragged around by the rotation.

372
00:20:35,097 --> 00:20:37,534
Second, it twists gyroscopes.

373
00:20:37,534 --> 00:20:41,872
A gyroscope's spin axis precesses at a rate with the shape of a dipole field,

374
00:20:41,872 --> 00:20:45,347
worked out by Lense and Thirring in 1918.

375
00:20:45,347 --> 00:20:49,284
Over the poles, the gyroscope is dragged along with the rotation;

376
00:20:49,284 --> 00:20:52,647
at the equator, the other way.

377
00:20:52,813 --> 00:20:56,988
There's a second, larger effect on gyroscopes, which doesn't need rotation

378
00:20:56,988 --> 00:20:57,888
at all.

379
00:20:57,888 --> 00:21:02,513
Send a gyroscope around a mass in a circular orbit, its spin axis pointing

380
00:21:02,513 --> 00:21:03,926
outward to start.

381
00:21:03,926 --> 00:21:07,801
A gyroscope is the best definition of a fixed direction we have:

382
00:21:07,801 --> 00:21:11,326
its spin is parallel transported along its worldline.

383
00:21:11,326 --> 00:21:15,263
Yet after one orbit, it no longer points where it started.

384
00:21:15,263 --> 00:21:19,838
Computed exactly, it has turned by two pi times one minus the square root

385
00:21:19,838 --> 00:21:23,876
of one minus three M over r, in the direction of the orbit:

386
00:21:23,876 --> 00:21:27,288
fifty-nine degrees per orbit at r equals ten M.

387
00:21:27,288 --> 00:21:33,788
This is geodetic precession, predicted by Willem de Sitter in 1916.

388
00:21:33,938 --> 00:21:35,476
Where does that turning come from?

389
00:21:35,476 --> 00:21:37,626
Part of it is the curvature of space.

390
00:21:37,626 --> 00:21:39,938
Here's Flamm's paraboloid from part one:

391
00:21:39,938 --> 00:21:42,876
the geometry of space around the mass at one moment.

392
00:21:42,876 --> 00:21:47,676
Transport a vector around the circle at r equals ten M, staying on the surface.

393
00:21:47,676 --> 00:21:50,376
Near that circle, the surface is like a cone.

394
00:21:50,376 --> 00:21:54,863
Cut the cone open and unroll it, and you get a flat sheet with a wedge missing.

395
00:21:54,863 --> 00:21:58,013
On the flat sheet, parallel transport is trivial:

396
00:21:58,013 --> 00:22:00,113
the arrows stay parallel.

397
00:22:00,113 --> 00:22:04,113
But when you glue the edges back together, the last arrow is turned relative

398
00:22:04,113 --> 00:22:08,651
to the first, by the angle of the missing wedge, thirty-eight degrees here.

399
00:22:08,651 --> 00:22:13,138
In weak gravity, curved space supplies exactly two thirds of the geodetic

400
00:22:13,138 --> 00:22:17,638
effect, and the warping of time the other third.

401
00:22:17,813 --> 00:22:20,051
Both effects were measured in orbit.

402
00:22:20,051 --> 00:22:24,638
Gravity Probe B, launched in 2004, carried four gyroscopes around the Earth

403
00:22:24,638 --> 00:22:27,788
in a polar orbit, pointed at a guide star.

404
00:22:27,788 --> 00:22:31,851
General relativity predicts that their axes should turn by six thousand six

405
00:22:31,851 --> 00:22:36,038
hundred milliarcseconds a year from geodetic precession, and by thirty-nine

406
00:22:36,038 --> 00:22:40,551
milliarcseconds a year, sideways, from the Earth's frame dragging:

407
00:22:40,551 --> 00:22:44,688
about the width of a human hair seen from half a kilometer away.

408
00:22:44,688 --> 00:22:45,676
The measurements:

409
00:22:45,676 --> 00:22:50,251
six thousand six hundred and two, plus or minus eighteen, and thirty-seven,

410
00:22:50,251 --> 00:22:52,138
plus or minus seven.

411
00:22:52,138 --> 00:22:56,026
Laser-tracked satellites see the Earth drag their orbits as well.

412
00:22:56,026 --> 00:22:59,288
Space really is dragged around by rotating mass.

413
00:23:00,878 --> 00:23:05,853
Real stars rotate, and a collapsing star spins faster and faster, like a skater

414
00:23:05,853 --> 00:23:07,378
pulling in their arms.

415
00:23:07,378 --> 00:23:09,353
So real black holes spin.

416
00:23:09,353 --> 00:23:13,903
The spacetime of a spinning black hole wasn't found until 1963, when Roy Kerr

417
00:23:13,903 --> 00:23:15,128
discovered this:

418
00:23:15,128 --> 00:23:19,803
the Kerr metric, with mass M and angular momentum J equal to M times a.

419
00:23:19,803 --> 00:23:24,628
Like Schwarzschild's, it's a solution of Einstein's equation in empty space;

420
00:23:24,628 --> 00:23:29,828
our symbolic code confirms that all ten components of its Ricci tensor vanish.

421
00:23:29,828 --> 00:23:32,078
With no spin, it's Schwarzschild.

422
00:23:32,078 --> 00:23:36,078
With no mass, it's flat spacetime in unusual coordinates.

423
00:23:36,078 --> 00:23:40,578
And far away, its time-space term is exactly the gravitomagnetic field we

424
00:23:40,578 --> 00:23:41,841
just derived.

425
00:23:41,841 --> 00:23:44,516
Remarkably, it's the only possibility:

426
00:23:44,516 --> 00:23:48,928
every stationary black hole in empty space is a Kerr black hole, described

427
00:23:48,928 --> 00:23:50,566
by just two numbers.

428
00:23:50,566 --> 00:23:53,778
Black holes have no hair.

429
00:23:53,903 --> 00:23:55,703
What does a Kerr black hole look like?

430
00:23:55,703 --> 00:23:58,603
Here's a slice through it, containing the spin axis.

431
00:23:58,603 --> 00:24:02,153
The horizon is where g r r blows up, where Delta vanishes.

432
00:24:02,153 --> 00:24:05,316
That happens at two radii, r plus and r minus.

433
00:24:05,316 --> 00:24:06,778
Let's turn up the spin.

434
00:24:06,778 --> 00:24:11,153
As a grows, the outer horizon shrinks, from two M toward M, and an inner horizon

435
00:24:11,153 --> 00:24:12,741
appears inside it.

436
00:24:12,741 --> 00:24:17,216
Outside the horizon there's a new surface, where g t t vanishes, which touches

437
00:24:17,216 --> 00:24:20,028
the horizon at the poles and bulges out at the equator:

438
00:24:20,028 --> 00:24:22,278
the boundary of the ergosphere.

439
00:24:22,278 --> 00:24:24,691
And the singularity is no longer a point:

440
00:24:24,691 --> 00:24:28,503
it's a ring, of radius a, here cut in two places.

441
00:24:28,503 --> 00:24:32,678
If a exceeded M, there'd be no horizon at all, and the singularity would be

442
00:24:32,678 --> 00:24:35,216
naked, visible from outside.

443
00:24:35,216 --> 00:24:38,566
Penrose conjectured that nature never allows that.

444
00:24:38,566 --> 00:24:43,278
This cosmic censorship conjecture is still unproven, but no generic process

445
00:24:43,278 --> 00:24:46,528
that violates it is known.

446
00:24:46,637 --> 00:24:50,237
The ergosphere is where frame dragging becomes irresistible.

447
00:24:50,237 --> 00:24:50,999
Ask:

448
00:24:50,999 --> 00:24:55,137
at a fixed radius, how fast can something orbit, as seen from far away?

449
00:24:55,137 --> 00:24:59,099
For its worldline to be timelike, slower than light, its angular velocity

450
00:24:59,099 --> 00:25:03,512
Omega has to make this quadratic negative, which confines it to a band.

451
00:25:03,512 --> 00:25:05,537
Far out, the band includes zero:

452
00:25:05,537 --> 00:25:08,637
you can orbit either way, or simply hover in place.

453
00:25:08,637 --> 00:25:12,212
At the ergosurface, the lower edge reaches zero.

454
00:25:12,212 --> 00:25:14,637
Inside, the whole band is positive:

455
00:25:14,637 --> 00:25:19,487
no rocket, however powerful, can hold you still relative to the distant stars.

456
00:25:19,487 --> 00:25:21,387
You must rotate with the hole.

457
00:25:21,387 --> 00:25:25,862
At the horizon, the band closes to a single value, Omega H:

458
00:25:25,862 --> 00:25:30,312
the horizon itself rotates rigidly at that rate.

459
00:25:30,462 --> 00:25:32,749
Here's a way to see the dragging directly.

460
00:25:32,749 --> 00:25:36,674
In the equatorial plane, set off a flash of light at each dot, and draw where

461
00:25:36,674 --> 00:25:38,637
the light has reached a moment later.

462
00:25:38,637 --> 00:25:42,024
Without spin, each flash is a circle around its source.

463
00:25:42,024 --> 00:25:43,412
Turn up the spin.

464
00:25:43,412 --> 00:25:47,312
The circles are swept around in the direction of rotation, most strongly near

465
00:25:47,312 --> 00:25:48,362
the hole.

466
00:25:48,362 --> 00:25:52,624
Inside the ergosurface, every circle is carried completely off its source:

467
00:25:52,624 --> 00:25:56,962
even light can't move against the rotation, or even stay put.

468
00:25:56,962 --> 00:26:00,187
That's why it's called the ergosphere, from the Greek for work:

469
00:26:00,187 --> 00:26:03,287
as we'll see next, energy can be extracted from it.

470
00:26:04,902 --> 00:26:08,302
The ergosphere allows something impossible anywhere else:

471
00:26:08,302 --> 00:26:09,865
negative energy.

472
00:26:09,865 --> 00:26:14,552
A particle's conserved energy, the one measured from far away, is minus its

473
00:26:14,552 --> 00:26:17,290
momentum dotted into the time direction.

474
00:26:17,290 --> 00:26:21,927
Split it into what a local observer, carried around by the dragging, measures,

475
00:26:21,927 --> 00:26:26,315
which is always positive, plus a term from frame dragging times the angular

476
00:26:26,315 --> 00:26:27,652
momentum.

477
00:26:27,652 --> 00:26:32,540
If the particle moves backward against the rotation, L is negative, and inside

478
00:26:32,540 --> 00:26:36,952
the ergosphere, the dragging is strong enough that the total can be negative.

479
00:26:36,952 --> 00:26:41,302
Geometrically, the time direction of the distant observers is spacelike there,

480
00:26:41,302 --> 00:26:44,140
so this dot product can have either sign.

481
00:26:44,140 --> 00:26:47,177
A particle on such an orbit can never escape.

482
00:26:47,177 --> 00:26:49,927
But it can fall in.

483
00:26:50,111 --> 00:26:53,598
Roger Penrose saw what this allows, in 1969.

484
00:26:53,598 --> 00:26:55,773
Send a particle into the ergosphere.

485
00:26:55,773 --> 00:27:00,473
There, let it split in two, throwing one piece backward, against the rotation,

486
00:27:00,473 --> 00:27:03,086
fast enough that its energy is negative.

487
00:27:03,086 --> 00:27:05,073
That piece falls into the hole.

488
00:27:05,073 --> 00:27:09,411
Energy is conserved, so the other piece leaves with more energy than the original

489
00:27:09,411 --> 00:27:10,961
particle brought in.

490
00:27:10,961 --> 00:27:14,636
In this computed example, almost ten percent more.

491
00:27:14,636 --> 00:27:18,136
The extra energy came from the black hole's rotation:

492
00:27:18,136 --> 00:27:22,586
swallowing negative energy and negative angular momentum, the hole gets a

493
00:27:22,586 --> 00:27:26,411
little lighter and spins a little slower.

494
00:27:26,536 --> 00:27:27,673
Is there a limit?

495
00:27:27,673 --> 00:27:31,136
Anything that crosses the horizon must move forward in time relative to the

496
00:27:31,136 --> 00:27:35,323
horizon's own light rays, which rotate with angular velocity Omega H.

497
00:27:35,323 --> 00:27:39,436
For the piece that falls in, that means E minus Omega H times L is at least

498
00:27:39,436 --> 00:27:40,348
zero.

499
00:27:40,348 --> 00:27:43,686
So the hole's mass can drop, but only by so much:

500
00:27:43,686 --> 00:27:47,461
the change in M is at least Omega H times the change in J.

501
00:27:47,461 --> 00:27:49,123
And here's a surprise.

502
00:27:49,123 --> 00:27:53,386
Differentiate the area of Kerr's horizon, and this inequality says exactly

503
00:27:53,386 --> 00:27:55,561
that the area can't decrease.

504
00:27:55,561 --> 00:27:59,886
Spin energy can be extracted, but the horizon's area only grows.

505
00:27:59,886 --> 00:28:02,586
We'll meet this again.

506
00:28:02,727 --> 00:28:06,415
Demetrios Christodoulou made this precise in 1970.

507
00:28:06,415 --> 00:28:09,202
A Kerr black hole's mass splits into two parts:

508
00:28:09,202 --> 00:28:13,990
an irreducible mass, fixed by the horizon's area, and the energy of its spin.

509
00:28:13,990 --> 00:28:16,990
Here are curves of constant area in the plane of J and M.

510
00:28:16,990 --> 00:28:21,927
A Penrose process, done as gently as possible, moves along one of these curves,

511
00:28:21,927 --> 00:28:25,977
removing spin and mass together, until the hole stops spinning.

512
00:28:25,977 --> 00:28:29,552
Starting from a maximally spinning hole, up to twenty-nine percent of its

513
00:28:29,552 --> 00:28:31,627
mass can be extracted.

514
00:28:31,627 --> 00:28:36,215
For comparison, nuclear fusion turns seven tenths of a percent of hydrogen's

515
00:28:36,215 --> 00:28:38,077
mass into energy.

516
00:28:38,077 --> 00:28:42,777
Nature's version probably uses magnetic fields threading the ergosphere, which

517
00:28:42,777 --> 00:28:46,177
may power the jets of quasars, like the one shooting out of M87.

518
00:28:48,759 --> 00:28:52,084
Spin also changes how close matter can orbit.

519
00:28:52,084 --> 00:28:55,822
In part one we found that around a non-spinning hole, the innermost stable

520
00:28:55,822 --> 00:28:57,559
circular orbit is at six M.

521
00:28:57,559 --> 00:29:02,059
Around a spinning one, orbits going the same way as the spin, prograde, can

522
00:29:02,059 --> 00:29:03,572
get much closer:

523
00:29:03,572 --> 00:29:07,309
down to M itself for a maximally spinning hole, while backward orbits are

524
00:29:07,309 --> 00:29:08,272
pushed out to nine M.

525
00:29:08,272 --> 00:29:13,184
That matters, because gas in an accretion disk spirals in through these orbits,

526
00:29:13,184 --> 00:29:17,309
radiating away its binding energy, until it reaches the innermost one and

527
00:29:17,309 --> 00:29:18,684
plunges.

528
00:29:18,684 --> 00:29:23,409
The fraction of its rest mass radiated is one minus the energy of that orbit:

529
00:29:23,409 --> 00:29:27,847
five point seven percent for a non-spinning hole, and up to forty-two percent

530
00:29:27,847 --> 00:29:29,947
for a maximally spinning one.

531
00:29:29,947 --> 00:29:34,197
Kip Thorne showed that accretion itself spins holes up to about a equals zero

532
00:29:34,197 --> 00:29:38,084
point nine nine eight, where the efficiency is thirty-two percent:

533
00:29:38,084 --> 00:29:41,959
the most efficient engines in the universe.

534
00:29:42,105 --> 00:29:44,180
What does a spinning black hole look like?

535
00:29:44,180 --> 00:29:47,793
The dark shadow is outlined by light rays that barely escape.

536
00:29:47,793 --> 00:29:52,705
In Kerr, light rays obey a separated radial equation, thanks to a hidden conserved

537
00:29:52,705 --> 00:29:56,493
quantity found by Brandon Carter in 1968.

538
00:29:56,493 --> 00:30:00,505
The critical rays are the ones that can circle forever at a fixed radius,

539
00:30:00,505 --> 00:30:03,255
where R and its derivative both vanish.

540
00:30:03,255 --> 00:30:07,318
Solving those two equations gives the ray's constants for every radius between

541
00:30:07,318 --> 00:30:10,555
the prograde and retrograde photon orbits.

542
00:30:10,555 --> 00:30:15,368
And each pair of constants marks one point on the sky of a distant observer.

543
00:30:15,368 --> 00:30:19,705
Together, they trace the edge of the shadow, as James Bardeen worked out in

544
00:30:19,705 --> 00:30:22,580
1973.

545
00:30:22,751 --> 00:30:25,301
Here's that edge for a black hole seen edge-on.

546
00:30:25,301 --> 00:30:29,751
Without spin, it's a circle, of radius the square root of twenty-seven M,

547
00:30:29,751 --> 00:30:31,651
as we found in part one.

548
00:30:31,651 --> 00:30:32,726
Spin it up.

549
00:30:32,726 --> 00:30:36,838
Photons orbiting with the rotation can get closer before being captured, so

550
00:30:36,838 --> 00:30:41,451
on that side the edge moves in and flattens, while the other side bulges out.

551
00:30:41,451 --> 00:30:46,126
Near maximal spin, the shadow becomes a letter D.

552
00:30:46,526 --> 00:30:48,651
Now the full picture, ray-traced.

553
00:30:48,651 --> 00:30:51,851
On the left, a non-spinning black hole, like part one's;

554
00:30:51,851 --> 00:30:55,526
on the right, the same disk and camera around a hole spinning at ninety-nine

555
00:30:55,526 --> 00:30:57,188
percent of the maximum.

556
00:30:57,188 --> 00:31:01,301
The disk reaches much closer in, because the innermost orbit has moved in,

557
00:31:01,301 --> 00:31:05,838
and the approaching side is brighter still, because the gas there moves faster.

558
00:31:05,838 --> 00:31:08,588
Overlay Bardeen's analytic shadow edge:

559
00:31:08,588 --> 00:31:12,701
it's exactly the boundary of the rays our tracer sees captured, pixel for

560
00:31:12,701 --> 00:31:15,001
pixel.

561
00:31:15,705 --> 00:31:20,568
Seen nearly face-on, which is roughly how we see the black hole in M87, the

562
00:31:20,568 --> 00:31:25,180
shadow is almost round whatever the spin, and its size changes by less than

563
00:31:25,180 --> 00:31:26,605
seven percent.

564
00:31:26,605 --> 00:31:30,455
That makes the shadow a good way to weigh a black hole, and a hard way to

565
00:31:30,455 --> 00:31:33,130
measure its spin.

566
00:31:33,830 --> 00:31:38,630
For the black hole in the galaxy M87, six and a half billion solar masses,

567
00:31:38,630 --> 00:31:43,893
fifty-five million light-years away, the shadow should span about forty micro-arcseconds.

568
00:31:43,893 --> 00:31:49,355
In 2019, the Event Horizon Telescope, a planet-sized array of radio dishes,

569
00:31:49,355 --> 00:31:54,268
imaged a bright ring forty-two micro-arcseconds across, around a dark center.

570
00:31:54,268 --> 00:32:00,355
In 2022 it imaged Sagittarius A star, at the center of our own galaxy.

571
00:32:00,355 --> 00:32:03,218
Both are consistent with Kerr black holes;

572
00:32:03,218 --> 00:32:07,405
models of the glowing gas favor spinning ones, but neither spin has been pinned

573
00:32:07,405 --> 00:32:08,230
down yet.

574
00:32:13,291 --> 00:32:17,691
In part one, we found that ripples in spacetime obey a wave equation, and

575
00:32:17,691 --> 00:32:20,603
quoted the formula for the waves a source makes.

576
00:32:20,603 --> 00:32:21,916
Let's derive it.

577
00:32:21,916 --> 00:32:24,578
Start from the linearized field equation.

578
00:32:24,578 --> 00:32:29,541
Like the equations of electromagnetism, it's solved by a retarded integral:

579
00:32:29,541 --> 00:32:33,691
the field here and now adds up the source everywhere, as it was when the signal

580
00:32:33,691 --> 00:32:34,941
left it.

581
00:32:34,941 --> 00:32:39,503
Far from a slowly moving source, the delay is the same for all of it, and

582
00:32:39,503 --> 00:32:44,053
the spatial part of the wave is four over r times the integral of the stresses,

583
00:32:44,053 --> 00:32:44,966
T i j.

584
00:32:44,966 --> 00:32:46,503
That looks hard to know:

585
00:32:46,503 --> 00:32:51,391
it involves pressures and stresses deep inside the source.

586
00:32:51,499 --> 00:32:53,811
Conservation comes to the rescue.

587
00:32:53,811 --> 00:32:56,161
Energy and momentum are conserved:

588
00:32:56,161 --> 00:33:01,374
two equations, one for the energy density and one for the momentum density.

589
00:33:01,374 --> 00:33:05,574
Differentiate the first with respect to time and substitute the second:

590
00:33:05,574 --> 00:33:09,486
the second time derivative of the energy density is a double divergence of

591
00:33:09,486 --> 00:33:10,749
the stresses.

592
00:33:10,749 --> 00:33:15,549
Now multiply by y i y j and integrate by parts, twice.

593
00:33:15,549 --> 00:33:19,311
The left side becomes the second time derivative of the mass distribution's

594
00:33:19,311 --> 00:33:20,349
second moment;

595
00:33:20,349 --> 00:33:23,599
the right side, twice the integrated stress.

596
00:33:23,599 --> 00:33:28,224
So the wave is two over r, times the second time derivative of the quadrupole

597
00:33:28,224 --> 00:33:29,186
moment.

598
00:33:29,186 --> 00:33:34,049
Only its traceless, transverse part is physical, which is the formula we quoted

599
00:33:34,049 --> 00:33:36,524
in part one.

600
00:33:36,657 --> 00:33:39,120
How much energy does the wave carry?

601
00:33:39,120 --> 00:33:44,270
Gravitational waves carry energy and momentum, with an effective energy density,

602
00:33:44,270 --> 00:33:48,532
averaged over a few wavelengths, given by this expression, due to Richard

603
00:33:48,532 --> 00:33:49,770
Isaacson.

604
00:33:49,770 --> 00:33:54,707
Integrate the flux over a big sphere around the source, and insert our wave.

605
00:33:54,707 --> 00:33:58,495
The angular integral of the transverse projection comes out to eight pi over

606
00:33:58,495 --> 00:34:03,307
five, which we checked numerically, and the result is the quadrupole formula:

607
00:34:03,307 --> 00:34:07,520
the luminosity is one fifth of the third time derivative of Q, squared.

608
00:34:07,520 --> 00:34:12,320
In everyday units, there's a factor G over c to the fifth, which is tiny.

609
00:34:12,320 --> 00:34:16,582
A huge steel beam spun almost to breaking radiates about ten to the minus

610
00:34:16,582 --> 00:34:17,995
twenty-nine watts.

611
00:34:17,995 --> 00:34:22,170
To radiate appreciably, you need stars' worth of mass moving at a good fraction

612
00:34:22,170 --> 00:34:24,782
of the speed of light.

613
00:34:24,974 --> 00:34:29,261
Now apply it to two stars, or black holes, in a circular orbit.

614
00:34:29,261 --> 00:34:33,361
The waves spiral out from the binary like water from a rotating sprinkler,

615
00:34:33,361 --> 00:34:35,686
here computed in the orbital plane.

616
00:34:35,686 --> 00:34:39,161
Pink is stretching in one direction, blue in the other.

617
00:34:39,161 --> 00:34:44,349
The quadrupole moment of the pair is mu a squared times n i n j, where n points

618
00:34:44,349 --> 00:34:46,374
along the line between them.

619
00:34:46,374 --> 00:34:50,374
It oscillates at twice the orbital frequency, because the pair looks the same

620
00:34:50,374 --> 00:34:54,386
after half a turn, and its third derivative squared is thirty-two mu squared

621
00:34:54,386 --> 00:34:56,574
a to the fourth omega to the sixth.

622
00:34:56,574 --> 00:35:00,886
With Kepler's law, the luminosity is thirty-two fifths mu squared M cubed

623
00:35:00,886 --> 00:35:02,236
over a to the fifth:

624
00:35:02,236 --> 00:35:04,861
closer orbits radiate ferociously.

625
00:35:04,861 --> 00:35:09,374
The radiation is strongest along the orbit's axis, eight times stronger than

626
00:35:09,374 --> 00:35:10,324
in its plane.

627
00:35:12,465 --> 00:35:15,115
The energy radiated has to come from somewhere:

628
00:35:15,115 --> 00:35:16,090
the orbit.

629
00:35:16,090 --> 00:35:19,240
The orbital energy of the pair is minus mu M over two a.

630
00:35:19,240 --> 00:35:22,615
It decreases at the rate the waves carry energy away.

631
00:35:22,615 --> 00:35:24,240
Solve for the separation:

632
00:35:24,240 --> 00:35:29,327
the orbit shrinks, slowly at first, and faster and faster as a gets smaller.

633
00:35:29,327 --> 00:35:33,652
Integrating, two bodies starting a distance a naught apart merge after five

634
00:35:33,652 --> 00:35:38,115
two-hundred-fifty-sixths of a naught to the fourth, over mu M squared.

635
00:35:38,115 --> 00:35:39,865
That fourth power is brutal:

636
00:35:39,865 --> 00:35:44,940
halve the distance, and the time to merge drops sixteen-fold.

637
00:35:45,065 --> 00:35:47,390
What a detector hears is the frequency.

638
00:35:47,390 --> 00:35:51,640
The gravitational-wave frequency is twice the orbital frequency, which Kepler's

639
00:35:51,640 --> 00:35:53,577
law ties to the separation.

640
00:35:53,577 --> 00:35:57,015
Differentiate it, substitute the shrinking rate, and eliminate a.

641
00:35:57,015 --> 00:35:58,765
Something remarkable happens:

642
00:35:58,765 --> 00:36:02,977
the two masses only appear in one combination, mu to the three fifths times

643
00:36:02,977 --> 00:36:05,977
M to the two fifths, called the chirp mass.

644
00:36:05,977 --> 00:36:10,665
Integrating gives the frequency as a function of the time left before coalescence.

645
00:36:10,665 --> 00:36:13,490
It rises without limit, a chirp.

646
00:36:13,490 --> 00:36:18,490
This is exactly the formula part one quoted, and laid over LIGO's data.

647
00:36:18,490 --> 00:36:23,865
Now we've derived it, five over two hundred fifty-six and all.

648
00:36:24,023 --> 00:36:28,135
Here it is for two black holes with the masses of LIGO's first detection.

649
00:36:28,135 --> 00:36:31,860
Two tenths of a second before they merge, they're a thousand kilometers apart

650
00:36:31,860 --> 00:36:34,048
and the wave is at thirty hertz.

651
00:36:34,048 --> 00:36:38,335
As they spiral together, the frequency and the amplitude both climb, faster

652
00:36:38,335 --> 00:36:39,473
and faster:

653
00:36:39,473 --> 00:36:40,535
the chirp.

654
00:36:40,535 --> 00:36:44,898
In the last instants, a few hundred kilometers apart, our leading-order formula

655
00:36:44,898 --> 00:36:49,235
breaks down, and only a full numerical solution of Einstein's equation can

656
00:36:49,235 --> 00:36:52,348
follow the merger itself.

657
00:36:52,465 --> 00:36:56,402
The first evidence that gravitational waves carry energy came from a pair

658
00:36:56,402 --> 00:36:58,052
of neutron stars.

659
00:36:58,052 --> 00:37:03,677
In 1974, Russell Hulse and Joseph Taylor found a pulsar in a binary orbit:

660
00:37:03,677 --> 00:37:08,702
two neutron stars, each about one point four solar masses, orbiting every

661
00:37:08,702 --> 00:37:12,965
seven and three quarter hours, one of them a spinning beacon that ticks seventeen

662
00:37:12,965 --> 00:37:15,790
times a second, a superb clock.

663
00:37:15,790 --> 00:37:20,227
The orbit is eccentric, so the quadrupole formula needs a correction, worked

664
00:37:20,227 --> 00:37:22,227
out by Peters and Mathews:

665
00:37:22,227 --> 00:37:26,277
it radiates almost twelve times more than a circular orbit would.

666
00:37:26,277 --> 00:37:27,415
The prediction:

667
00:37:27,415 --> 00:37:31,365
the orbital period should shrink by two point four trillionths of a second,

668
00:37:31,365 --> 00:37:33,940
every second.

669
00:37:34,156 --> 00:37:38,294
A shrinking period shows up as a drift in the time of closest approach, growing

670
00:37:38,294 --> 00:37:40,494
like the square of the elapsed time.

671
00:37:40,494 --> 00:37:44,231
Here's general relativity's prediction since 1974:

672
00:37:44,231 --> 00:37:46,669
by now, more than a hundred seconds.

673
00:37:46,669 --> 00:37:50,269
The measured decay, after correcting for the acceleration of the pulsar in

674
00:37:50,269 --> 00:37:54,419
our galaxy, agrees with the prediction to two parts in a thousand.

675
00:37:54,419 --> 00:37:57,944
The orbit's ellipse also turns, by four degrees a year:

676
00:37:57,944 --> 00:38:01,156
Mercury's effect, in a far stronger field.

677
00:38:01,156 --> 00:38:05,869
And a second system, the Double Pulsar, now agrees with the quadrupole formula

678
00:38:05,869 --> 00:38:08,344
to a few parts in a hundred thousand.

679
00:38:08,344 --> 00:38:12,694
In about three hundred million years, these two stars will merge, ending with

680
00:38:12,694 --> 00:38:14,781
a chirp like the ones LIGO hears.

681
00:38:16,395 --> 00:38:20,770
When two black holes merge, the result is a single, distorted black hole,

682
00:38:20,770 --> 00:38:23,858
which settles down by ringing, like a struck bell.

683
00:38:23,858 --> 00:38:28,020
Small ripples of a black hole obey a wave equation in the tortoise coordinate,

684
00:38:28,020 --> 00:38:33,108
found by Tullio Regge and John Wheeler in 1957, with a potential barrier.

685
00:38:33,108 --> 00:38:37,245
The barrier peaks just outside the photon sphere, where light can orbit:

686
00:38:37,245 --> 00:38:40,970
waves near the hole are partly trapped there, leaking slowly inward to the

687
00:38:40,970 --> 00:38:44,295
horizon and outward to us.

688
00:38:44,449 --> 00:38:45,787
Let's watch it happen.

689
00:38:45,787 --> 00:38:49,574
Send a pulse of gravitational waves toward the barrier, and solve the equation

690
00:38:49,574 --> 00:38:50,762
numerically.

691
00:38:50,762 --> 00:38:53,987
Part of the pulse crosses the barrier and falls into the hole.

692
00:38:53,987 --> 00:38:57,524
Part bounces back, and what comes back isn't a copy of the pulse:

693
00:38:57,524 --> 00:39:01,049
it's a ringing, at a definite frequency, dying away.

694
00:39:01,049 --> 00:39:04,874
On a logarithmic scale, the ringing's envelope is a straight line:

695
00:39:04,874 --> 00:39:10,399
an exponential decay, until a faint power-law tail takes over.

696
00:39:10,570 --> 00:39:14,558
Fitting the ringing in our simulation gives a frequency and a damping rate.

697
00:39:14,558 --> 00:39:19,395
They match, to a fraction of a percent, the exact quasinormal frequency, computed

698
00:39:19,395 --> 00:39:23,120
by a completely different method, Edward Leaver's continued fraction from

699
00:39:23,120 --> 00:39:24,695
1985.

700
00:39:24,695 --> 00:39:26,770
And there's a simple picture behind it:

701
00:39:26,770 --> 00:39:31,270
the ringing is waves trapped near the photon sphere, so its frequency is roughly

702
00:39:31,270 --> 00:39:35,145
the orbital frequency of light there, and it leaks away at the rate light

703
00:39:35,145 --> 00:39:37,320
orbits there are unstable.

704
00:39:37,320 --> 00:39:38,620
The crucial point:

705
00:39:38,620 --> 00:39:42,295
the frequencies depend only on the final mass and spin.

706
00:39:42,295 --> 00:39:46,245
Measure them, and you test whether the remnant really is a Kerr black hole

707
00:39:46,245 --> 00:39:47,520
with no hair.

708
00:39:47,520 --> 00:39:51,783
For the black hole left by LIGO's first detection, the main tone is at about

709
00:39:51,783 --> 00:39:57,145
two hundred and fifty hertz, dying away in four milliseconds.

710
00:39:57,295 --> 00:40:01,495
Here are the last cycles of LIGO's first signal, the real data.

711
00:40:01,495 --> 00:40:05,895
After the peak, lay on a ringing with the frequency and damping time predicted

712
00:40:05,895 --> 00:40:10,158
for the final black hole, fitting only its amplitude and phase.

713
00:40:10,158 --> 00:40:14,495
The signal is faint and noisy here, but the tail end of the collision rings

714
00:40:14,495 --> 00:40:17,995
just as a Kerr black hole should.

715
00:40:18,212 --> 00:40:22,837
In 1971, Stephen Hawking proved that this kind of collision can only make

716
00:40:22,837 --> 00:40:24,424
black holes bigger.

717
00:40:24,424 --> 00:40:28,549
The horizon is made of light rays, and Raychaudhuri's equation applies to

718
00:40:28,549 --> 00:40:29,287
them.

719
00:40:29,287 --> 00:40:33,424
If they ever started converging, they'd meet within a finite distance.

720
00:40:33,424 --> 00:40:37,887
But light rays that meet leave the horizon, and the horizon's light rays never

721
00:40:37,887 --> 00:40:38,724
leave it.

722
00:40:38,724 --> 00:40:43,012
So they never converge, and the horizon's area never decreases.

723
00:40:43,012 --> 00:40:46,112
That limits how much energy a merger can radiate:

724
00:40:46,112 --> 00:40:50,787
for two equal non-spinning holes, at most twenty-nine percent of their mass.

725
00:40:50,787 --> 00:40:55,837
LIGO's first event radiated about five percent, three solar masses' worth,

726
00:40:55,837 --> 00:40:58,637
in a fifth of a second.

727
00:40:58,828 --> 00:41:03,691
In January 2025, LIGO caught the loudest black-hole merger yet, two holes

728
00:41:03,691 --> 00:41:06,203
of about thirty-three solar masses each.

729
00:41:06,203 --> 00:41:09,428
From the inspiral, you can infer the areas of the two horizons before the

730
00:41:09,428 --> 00:41:10,003
merger;

731
00:41:10,003 --> 00:41:11,978
from the ringdown, the area after.

732
00:41:11,978 --> 00:41:12,666
Before:

733
00:41:12,666 --> 00:41:15,816
two hundred and thirty-seven thousand square kilometers.

734
00:41:15,816 --> 00:41:16,391
After:

735
00:41:16,391 --> 00:41:18,616
three hundred and seventy-three thousand.

736
00:41:18,616 --> 00:41:22,516
The collaboration found the area increased, with a confidence of four point

737
00:41:22,516 --> 00:41:23,541
four sigma:

738
00:41:23,541 --> 00:41:27,528
Hawking's area theorem, tested on real black holes.

739
00:41:27,528 --> 00:41:31,866
And the ringdown was loud enough to hear the main tone and its first overtone,

740
00:41:31,866 --> 00:41:36,820
at the frequencies a Kerr black hole of that mass and spin should have.

741
00:41:36,820 --> 00:41:38,883
An area that can only grow.

742
00:41:38,883 --> 00:41:42,458
That's a lot like another quantity in physics that can only grow:

743
00:41:42,458 --> 00:41:43,420
entropy.

744
00:41:43,420 --> 00:41:45,420
Is the resemblance a coincidence?

745
00:41:50,518 --> 00:41:55,993
In 1972, Jacob Bekenstein, a graduate student of John Wheeler's, asked a simple

746
00:41:55,993 --> 00:41:57,056
question.

747
00:41:57,056 --> 00:42:01,293
Take a box of hot gas, which has entropy, and drop it into a black hole.

748
00:42:01,293 --> 00:42:05,743
From the outside, the gas and its entropy are simply gone, so the total entropy

749
00:42:05,743 --> 00:42:10,931
of the universe outside has gone down, violating the second law of thermodynamics.

750
00:42:10,931 --> 00:42:15,668
Unless the black hole itself has entropy, which grows when things fall in.

751
00:42:15,668 --> 00:42:17,643
And there was an obvious candidate:

752
00:42:17,643 --> 00:42:21,793
the one property of a black hole that, like entropy, can only increase.

753
00:42:21,793 --> 00:42:24,193
Its area.

754
00:42:24,293 --> 00:42:27,381
If area is like entropy, what's like temperature?

755
00:42:27,381 --> 00:42:29,543
Hover a rocket above a black hole.

756
00:42:29,543 --> 00:42:33,881
The acceleration it needs grows without bound as it nears the horizon.

757
00:42:33,881 --> 00:42:39,006
But measured from far away, redshifted by the same factor that slows its clocks,

758
00:42:39,006 --> 00:42:41,031
it tends to a finite limit:

759
00:42:41,031 --> 00:42:41,993
one over four M.

760
00:42:41,993 --> 00:42:44,743
This is the surface gravity, kappa.

761
00:42:44,743 --> 00:42:48,543
For a spinning hole it's this, and it turns out to be the same everywhere

762
00:42:48,543 --> 00:42:53,356
on the horizon of any stationary black hole, just as temperature is the same

763
00:42:53,356 --> 00:42:57,018
everywhere in a body in equilibrium.

764
00:42:57,126 --> 00:42:58,876
The analogy goes deeper.

765
00:42:58,876 --> 00:43:03,789
The area of a Kerr horizon is a function of the hole's mass and angular momentum.

766
00:43:03,789 --> 00:43:04,976
Differentiate it:

767
00:43:04,976 --> 00:43:09,139
the partial derivatives are exactly the surface gravity over eight pi, and

768
00:43:09,139 --> 00:43:11,576
the horizon's angular velocity.

769
00:43:11,576 --> 00:43:15,589
So a small change in mass splits into kappa over eight pi times the change

770
00:43:15,589 --> 00:43:20,051
in area, plus Omega H times the change in angular momentum:

771
00:43:20,051 --> 00:43:22,226
the work done spinning it up.

772
00:43:22,226 --> 00:43:25,014
Compare the first law of thermodynamics:

773
00:43:25,014 --> 00:43:29,314
energy changes by temperature times entropy change, plus work.

774
00:43:29,314 --> 00:43:30,951
Temperature goes with kappa;

775
00:43:30,951 --> 00:43:33,951
entropy goes with area.

776
00:43:34,110 --> 00:43:38,710
In 1973, James Bardeen, Brandon Carter and Stephen Hawking laid out the full

777
00:43:38,710 --> 00:43:39,822
parallel.

778
00:43:39,822 --> 00:43:40,947
The zeroth law:

779
00:43:40,947 --> 00:43:45,335
surface gravity is uniform on the horizon, like temperature in equilibrium.

780
00:43:45,335 --> 00:43:47,410
The first law we've just derived.

781
00:43:47,410 --> 00:43:49,710
The second law is the area theorem.

782
00:43:49,710 --> 00:43:50,610
And the third:

783
00:43:50,610 --> 00:43:54,647
you can't reduce kappa to zero, an extremal black hole, in a finite number

784
00:43:54,647 --> 00:43:57,710
of steps, just as you can't reach absolute zero.

785
00:43:57,710 --> 00:44:01,210
But there was a catch, and Hawking himself insisted on it.

786
00:44:01,210 --> 00:44:03,572
A body with a temperature radiates.

787
00:44:03,572 --> 00:44:08,535
A black hole, classically, absorbs everything and emits nothing, so its temperature

788
00:44:08,535 --> 00:44:09,860
must be zero.

789
00:44:09,860 --> 00:44:11,710
The analogy looked like just that:

790
00:44:11,710 --> 00:44:12,685
an analogy.

791
00:44:14,342 --> 00:44:19,342
In 1974, Hawking applied quantum field theory to a collapsing star, expecting

792
00:44:19,342 --> 00:44:22,042
to confirm that black holes are perfectly black.

793
00:44:22,042 --> 00:44:23,330
He found the opposite.

794
00:44:23,330 --> 00:44:24,655
Here's the heart of it.

795
00:44:24,655 --> 00:44:27,805
Look at light rays leaving from just outside the horizon.

796
00:44:27,805 --> 00:44:31,705
A ray starting a tenth of the horizon radius out escapes quickly.

797
00:44:31,705 --> 00:44:36,142
One starting a hundred times closer takes four M times the log of ten longer,

798
00:44:36,142 --> 00:44:37,205
and so on:

799
00:44:37,205 --> 00:44:41,080
every factor of ten closer delays the escape by the same amount.

800
00:44:41,080 --> 00:44:42,792
Turn that around.

801
00:44:42,792 --> 00:44:47,592
The light reaching a distant observer late, at time u, left from an exponentially

802
00:44:47,592 --> 00:44:49,792
thin layer near the horizon.

803
00:44:49,792 --> 00:44:54,192
Rays arriving later and later trace back to a region squeezed by a factor

804
00:44:54,192 --> 00:44:56,017
of e every four M:

805
00:44:56,017 --> 00:45:00,167
the past position of a ray depends exponentially on when it arrives, with

806
00:45:00,167 --> 00:45:04,067
rate kappa, the surface gravity.

807
00:45:04,184 --> 00:45:05,296
Why does that matter?

808
00:45:05,296 --> 00:45:08,859
In quantum field theory, what counts as a particle depends on what counts

809
00:45:08,859 --> 00:45:12,896
as a positive-frequency wave, and that depends on whose clock you use.

810
00:45:12,896 --> 00:45:16,809
A wave that oscillates smoothly in the past reaches the distant observer stretched

811
00:45:16,809 --> 00:45:18,046
exponentially:

812
00:45:18,046 --> 00:45:20,296
rapid at first, then frozen.

813
00:45:20,296 --> 00:45:23,834
Take its Fourier transform with respect to the observer's time.

814
00:45:23,834 --> 00:45:26,496
It isn't purely positive frequency any more:

815
00:45:26,496 --> 00:45:30,559
it contains negative frequencies too, which means particles have been created

816
00:45:30,559 --> 00:45:32,109
from the vacuum.

817
00:45:32,109 --> 00:45:36,634
And the ratio of negative to positive is exactly e to the minus two pi omega

818
00:45:36,634 --> 00:45:37,834
over kappa.

819
00:45:37,834 --> 00:45:40,209
We computed the transform numerically:

820
00:45:40,209 --> 00:45:42,171
the dots fall right on the line.

821
00:45:42,171 --> 00:45:44,009
That's a Boltzmann factor.

822
00:45:44,009 --> 00:45:48,084
The particles come out with a thermal, Planck spectrum, at temperature kappa

823
00:45:48,084 --> 00:45:49,384
over two pi:

824
00:45:49,384 --> 00:45:52,584
one over eight pi M.

825
00:45:52,717 --> 00:45:56,480
There's a second, astonishingly short route to the same temperature.

826
00:45:56,480 --> 00:45:59,005
Measure distance from the horizon with rho.

827
00:45:59,005 --> 00:46:03,330
Near the horizon, the metric becomes flat spacetime as described by accelerated

828
00:46:03,330 --> 00:46:04,567
observers.

829
00:46:04,567 --> 00:46:06,080
Now do something strange:

830
00:46:06,080 --> 00:46:07,742
make time imaginary.

831
00:46:07,742 --> 00:46:12,392
The metric becomes rho squared times d tau over four M squared, plus d rho

832
00:46:12,392 --> 00:46:16,992
squared, which is the flat plane in polar coordinates, with tau over four

833
00:46:16,992 --> 00:46:18,850
M as the angle.

834
00:46:18,850 --> 00:46:22,963
For the plane to be smooth at the origin, the angle has to go around exactly

835
00:46:22,963 --> 00:46:27,275
two pi, so imaginary time must repeat with period eight pi M.

836
00:46:27,275 --> 00:46:29,825
Otherwise the origin is the tip of a cone.

837
00:46:29,825 --> 00:46:34,150
And in quantum statistical mechanics, a system whose fields are periodic in

838
00:46:34,150 --> 00:46:39,088
imaginary time, with period h-bar over k T, is at temperature T.

839
00:46:39,088 --> 00:46:44,350
So a black hole has temperature one over eight pi M, the same answer, as Gibbons

840
00:46:44,350 --> 00:46:47,350
and Hawking showed in 1977.

841
00:46:47,350 --> 00:46:51,925
With the constants put back, that's this formula, which is carved on Hawking's

842
00:46:51,925 --> 00:46:55,775
memorial stone in Westminster Abbey.

843
00:46:55,917 --> 00:47:00,642
Here's that geometry, the plane of r and imaginary time, embedded exactly

844
00:47:00,642 --> 00:47:01,942
as a surface.

845
00:47:01,942 --> 00:47:03,805
Far away, it's a cylinder:

846
00:47:03,805 --> 00:47:06,142
imaginary time going around.

847
00:47:06,142 --> 00:47:08,342
At the horizon, it closes off.

848
00:47:08,342 --> 00:47:12,242
With the right period, the tip is smooth, like the end of a cigar.

849
00:47:12,242 --> 00:47:15,317
With any other period, it ends in a sharp cone.

850
00:47:15,317 --> 00:47:19,267
The geometry itself picks out the temperature.

851
00:47:19,392 --> 00:47:20,655
Put the numbers in.

852
00:47:20,655 --> 00:47:24,117
A black hole of one solar mass has a temperature of sixty billionths of a

853
00:47:24,117 --> 00:47:26,780
kelvin, and heavier ones are colder still:

854
00:47:26,780 --> 00:47:30,855
far colder than the cosmic microwave background, so every black hole we know

855
00:47:30,855 --> 00:47:32,905
of absorbs more than it emits.

856
00:47:32,905 --> 00:47:36,367
Only one lighter than about sixty percent of the Moon's mass would be hotter

857
00:47:36,367 --> 00:47:37,892
than the sky around it.

858
00:47:37,892 --> 00:47:39,805
The temperature fixes the entropy:

859
00:47:39,805 --> 00:47:42,992
one quarter of the horizon area, in Planck units.

860
00:47:42,992 --> 00:47:46,817
Bekenstein's guess was right, with the factor now fixed.

861
00:47:46,817 --> 00:47:51,380
For a solar-mass black hole, that's ten to the seventy-seven, about ten billion

862
00:47:51,380 --> 00:47:54,167
billion times the entropy of the Sun itself.

863
00:47:54,167 --> 00:47:56,655
And a body that radiates loses mass:

864
00:47:56,655 --> 00:48:01,830
left alone, a black hole evaporates, in a time proportional to its mass cubed,

865
00:48:01,830 --> 00:48:05,330
around ten to the sixty-seven years for a solar mass.

866
00:48:05,330 --> 00:48:08,880
Whether the information that fell in comes back out in the radiation, and

867
00:48:08,880 --> 00:48:12,692
how, is still one of the deepest open questions in physics.

868
00:48:17,973 --> 00:48:19,873
Finally, the biggest solution of all:

869
00:48:19,873 --> 00:48:21,023
the universe.

870
00:48:21,023 --> 00:48:24,598
On the largest scales, the universe looks the same everywhere and in every

871
00:48:24,598 --> 00:48:25,648
direction.

872
00:48:25,648 --> 00:48:30,048
If that's exactly true, space must have the same curvature at every point,

873
00:48:30,048 --> 00:48:32,386
and there are only three possibilities.

874
00:48:32,386 --> 00:48:34,411
Positive curvature, like a sphere:

875
00:48:34,411 --> 00:48:38,973
a closed universe, where triangles have more than one hundred eighty degrees.

876
00:48:38,973 --> 00:48:40,111
Zero curvature:

877
00:48:40,111 --> 00:48:41,673
flat space.

878
00:48:41,673 --> 00:48:47,023
Or negative curvature, a hyperbolic space, drawn here in Poincaré's disk,

879
00:48:47,023 --> 00:48:51,386
where triangles have less than one hundred eighty degrees, and the circumference

880
00:48:51,386 --> 00:48:55,948
of a circle grows exponentially with its radius.

881
00:48:56,107 --> 00:49:00,069
Space with constant curvature, multiplied by a scale factor a that can change

882
00:49:00,069 --> 00:49:01,119
with time:

883
00:49:01,119 --> 00:49:06,069
this is the Friedmann–Lemaître–Robertson–Walker metric, where k is plus one,

884
00:49:06,069 --> 00:49:07,932
zero, or minus one.

885
00:49:07,932 --> 00:49:11,832
Galaxies sit at fixed coordinates, and all distances between them grow in

886
00:49:11,832 --> 00:49:13,057
proportion to a of t.

887
00:49:13,057 --> 00:49:17,532
From any one galaxy, all the others recede, at speeds proportional to their

888
00:49:17,532 --> 00:49:18,569
distance:

889
00:49:18,569 --> 00:49:23,232
Hubble's law, with the Hubble rate a dot over a.

890
00:49:23,390 --> 00:49:25,665
Now run the machine from part one.

891
00:49:25,665 --> 00:49:27,140
The Christoffel symbols:

892
00:49:27,140 --> 00:49:30,515
the Ricci tensor, computed and checked symbolically.

893
00:49:30,515 --> 00:49:34,640
Fill the universe with a perfect fluid, at rest relative to the galaxies,

894
00:49:34,640 --> 00:49:38,103
and take the time-time component of Einstein's equation.

895
00:49:38,103 --> 00:49:39,503
Divide by three:

896
00:49:39,503 --> 00:49:41,590
this is the Friedmann equation.

897
00:49:41,590 --> 00:49:46,390
The expansion rate squared is set by the density, the curvature, and the cosmological

898
00:49:46,390 --> 00:49:47,890
constant.

899
00:49:47,890 --> 00:49:51,765
The space-space components give the acceleration equation, which we found

900
00:49:51,765 --> 00:49:55,515
in part one from Baez and Bunn's ball of coffee grounds.

901
00:49:55,515 --> 00:50:00,590
And energy conservation says how each ingredient thins out as space expands:

902
00:50:00,590 --> 00:50:05,228
matter as one over a cubed, radiation as one over a to the fourth, because

903
00:50:05,228 --> 00:50:10,665
each photon is also stretched, and vacuum energy not at all.

904
00:50:10,815 --> 00:50:14,140
Here are the three ingredients of our universe, with their measured amounts,

905
00:50:14,140 --> 00:50:15,928
against the scale factor.

906
00:50:15,928 --> 00:50:20,853
Radiation dominated early on, until the universe was about a three-thousand-four-hundredth

907
00:50:20,853 --> 00:50:22,428
of its present size.

908
00:50:22,428 --> 00:50:26,765
Then matter took over, and only recently, at a redshift of about a third,

909
00:50:26,765 --> 00:50:30,003
did the cosmological constant overtake matter.

910
00:50:30,003 --> 00:50:34,040
The Friedmann equation also says how much density makes space flat:

911
00:50:34,040 --> 00:50:38,640
the critical density, about five protons per cubic meter today.

912
00:50:38,640 --> 00:50:42,678
The departure from it measures curvature, and our universe turns out to be

913
00:50:42,678 --> 00:50:46,465
flat to within a fraction of a percent.

914
00:50:46,640 --> 00:50:50,903
Solve the Friedmann equation, and each mix of ingredients gives its own history.

915
00:50:50,903 --> 00:50:54,178
All of these universes expand at today's measured rate.

916
00:50:54,178 --> 00:50:58,203
A closed universe of matter expands, stops, and recollapses.

917
00:50:58,203 --> 00:51:00,790
A flat one of matter alone keeps slowing forever;

918
00:51:00,790 --> 00:51:02,478
an open one coasts.

919
00:51:02,478 --> 00:51:06,165
A universe of pure vacuum energy expands exponentially.

920
00:51:06,165 --> 00:51:10,915
Ours, matter plus a cosmological constant, decelerated for its first seven

921
00:51:10,915 --> 00:51:15,415
billion years, and now accelerates, heading toward the exponential.

922
00:51:15,415 --> 00:51:19,765
Einstein himself had wanted a static universe, and added the cosmological

923
00:51:19,765 --> 00:51:21,315
constant to hold it up;

924
00:51:21,315 --> 00:51:23,728
but that balance is unstable.

925
00:51:23,728 --> 00:51:28,778
Alexander Friedmann saw in 1922 that the equation demands a universe that

926
00:51:28,778 --> 00:51:33,390
changes, and in 1929 Edwin Hubble found that it expands.

927
00:51:35,046 --> 00:51:36,996
How do we know the universe expands?

928
00:51:36,996 --> 00:51:38,009
Through light.

929
00:51:38,009 --> 00:51:42,409
A light ray moving through flat, expanding space covers a comoving distance

930
00:51:42,409 --> 00:51:44,721
d t over a in each moment.

931
00:51:44,721 --> 00:51:49,159
Two successive crests of a light wave, sent a moment apart, travel the same

932
00:51:49,159 --> 00:51:50,759
comoving distance.

933
00:51:50,759 --> 00:51:54,684
So the interval between them grows in proportion to the scale factor.

934
00:51:54,684 --> 00:51:58,934
The wavelength stretches by exactly the factor the universe expanded while

935
00:51:58,934 --> 00:52:00,796
the light was in flight.

936
00:52:00,796 --> 00:52:02,921
That's the cosmological redshift:

937
00:52:02,921 --> 00:52:08,446
not a Doppler shift through space, but space itself stretching the wave.

938
00:52:08,630 --> 00:52:12,630
Expansion makes distances and horizons subtle, and one picture sorts them

939
00:52:12,630 --> 00:52:13,392
out.

940
00:52:13,392 --> 00:52:17,617
Time goes up, distance from us across, computed for our universe.

941
00:52:17,617 --> 00:52:20,530
Galaxies drift apart along these grey lines.

942
00:52:20,530 --> 00:52:25,180
Our past light cone, everything we can see now, is this yellow teardrop:

943
00:52:25,180 --> 00:52:28,605
light that reaches us from the most distant sources was emitted when those

944
00:52:28,605 --> 00:52:32,730
sources were close to us, and the expansion then carried the light away before

945
00:52:32,730 --> 00:52:34,855
it could make headway toward us.

946
00:52:34,855 --> 00:52:38,780
The teal curve is the Hubble sphere, where galaxies recede at the speed of

947
00:52:38,780 --> 00:52:39,705
light.

948
00:52:39,705 --> 00:52:44,305
Galaxies beyond it recede faster than light, and we still see many of them:

949
00:52:44,305 --> 00:52:46,755
nothing moves faster than light locally;

950
00:52:46,755 --> 00:52:49,596
space between us is expanding.

951
00:52:49,596 --> 00:52:53,046
Now divide out the expansion, and use comoving distance.

952
00:52:53,046 --> 00:52:56,696
And finally, replace time by conformal time, the distance light could have

953
00:52:56,696 --> 00:52:57,684
traveled.

954
00:52:57,684 --> 00:53:01,721
In these coordinates, light moves at forty-five degrees again, like in a Penrose

955
00:53:01,721 --> 00:53:02,871
diagram.

956
00:53:02,871 --> 00:53:06,834
The particle horizon, the farthest anything we see could be today, is forty-six

957
00:53:06,834 --> 00:53:08,596
billion light-years away.

958
00:53:08,596 --> 00:53:10,084
And the event horizon:

959
00:53:10,084 --> 00:53:13,896
events beyond it today, sixteen and a half billion light-years away, will

960
00:53:13,896 --> 00:53:18,821
never be seen by us, because the expansion is accelerating.

961
00:53:18,946 --> 00:53:22,859
The farthest light we can see is the cosmic microwave background, released

962
00:53:22,859 --> 00:53:26,696
when the universe became transparent, a few hundred thousand years after the

963
00:53:26,696 --> 00:53:27,821
Big Bang.

964
00:53:27,821 --> 00:53:31,571
The places that emitted it now lie on a sphere forty-five billion light-years

965
00:53:31,571 --> 00:53:32,471
away.

966
00:53:32,471 --> 00:53:35,884
But at the moment of emission, each of those places could only have received

967
00:53:35,884 --> 00:53:39,309
signals from a tiny region, drawn here to scale:

968
00:53:39,309 --> 00:53:42,184
about a degree across, as seen from Earth.

969
00:53:42,184 --> 00:53:46,146
So the sky contains some ten thousand patches that had never been in causal

970
00:53:46,146 --> 00:53:47,546
contact.

971
00:53:47,546 --> 00:53:51,884
Yet they all have the same temperature, to one part in a hundred thousand.

972
00:53:51,884 --> 00:53:53,059
How did they know?

973
00:53:53,059 --> 00:53:58,109
This is the horizon problem, and the leading proposed answer, inflation, adds

974
00:53:58,109 --> 00:54:04,121
an early epoch of exponential expansion, before the history we've drawn.

975
00:54:04,288 --> 00:54:07,513
These equations are still being tested at the largest scales.

976
00:54:07,513 --> 00:54:11,688
The expansion rate inferred from the microwave background, assuming this model,

977
00:54:11,688 --> 00:54:15,376
is about sixty-seven kilometers per second per megaparsec.

978
00:54:15,376 --> 00:54:19,901
Measured directly from nearby supernovae, it's about seventy-three and a half.

979
00:54:19,901 --> 00:54:24,488
The disagreement, now at five to seven sigma, is called the Hubble tension.

980
00:54:24,488 --> 00:54:28,663
And maps of millions of galaxies from the DESI survey hint that dark energy

981
00:54:28,663 --> 00:54:32,888
may change over time, which a cosmological constant can't.

982
00:54:32,888 --> 00:54:38,788
The evidence isn't yet decisive, and DESI's full results are expected in 2027.

983
00:54:40,370 --> 00:54:41,820
Let's put it together.

984
00:54:41,820 --> 00:54:45,645
Choosing coordinates that follow light showed us that the horizon is an ordinary

985
00:54:45,645 --> 00:54:49,682
place to cross, and that inside, r becomes time.

986
00:54:49,682 --> 00:54:54,070
Raychaudhuri's equation showed that gravity always focuses, so once a trapped

987
00:54:54,070 --> 00:54:57,445
surface forms, a singularity is unavoidable.

988
00:54:57,445 --> 00:55:01,582
Spinning black holes drag spacetime around them, and store energy that can

989
00:55:01,582 --> 00:55:04,845
be mined, while their area only grows.

990
00:55:04,845 --> 00:55:09,482
Gravitational waves carry energy away, and from that one fact we derived the

991
00:55:09,482 --> 00:55:14,032
chirp of merging black holes and the slow death of the binary pulsar.

992
00:55:14,032 --> 00:55:18,595
Quantum theory turned the laws of black-hole mechanics into real thermodynamics,

993
00:55:18,595 --> 00:55:20,820
with a temperature and an entropy.

994
00:55:20,820 --> 00:55:25,095
And the same equation, applied to everything at once, describes an expanding

995
00:55:25,095 --> 00:55:28,970
universe, with horizons of its own.

996
00:55:29,170 --> 00:55:33,232
All of it from one line, derived from a falling elevator, and reaching to

997
00:55:33,232 --> 00:55:35,557
the edge of the observable universe.

998
00:55:35,557 --> 00:55:36,820
Thanks for watching.
